From: DroidDeck-Mali, after wonderkast02/panvk-g720-kbase-csf 0.1.0-beta.2
Date: 2026-10-14
Subject: CSF: size the command-stream register file from the kbase uAPI

The kbase backend reported a fixed 96 CS registers because its own comment
called the stream-feature encoding unreliable: a Pixel 7 kbase 1.38 reports a
work-register value below the register file the CS compiler was sized against.
That reasoning holds for the under-reporting case but throws away the one
signal that says a bigger file exists. A 5th-gen part (arch >= 12) has 128 CS
registers - this tree's own drm-shim says so at
src/panfrost/drm-shim/panfrost_noop.c:203 - and a 96-register allocation
under-runs it, which is the register-count defect wonderkast02's
0001-panvk-kbase-g720-register-count.patch fixes on a Mali-G720.

Take the larger of the reported count (stream features [7:0], the same
WORK_REGS-1 encoding panthor's kernel driver reads) and the 96 that panthor
assumes for everything below 5th-gen, and cap it at the 128 a 5th-gen file
has. A stale under-report still floors at 96, a 5th-gen report now reaches
128, and a bogus high report cannot make the allocator overrun the file.

kbase_subqueue_emit_job() also built its CS builder without nr_kernel_registers
where every other builder in the tree sets it to
MAX2(unpreserved_cs_reg_count, 4). cs_builder_init() floors that to 3, so the
allocator was free to hand out one register the kernel clobbers between
submissions.

diff --git a/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c b/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c
index 6908f71..09caa7d 100644
--- a/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c
+++ b/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c
@@ -495,14 +495,15 @@ kbase_query_csif_info(int fd, struct drm_panthor_csif_info *csif)
       return -1;
    }
 
-   /* Stream features nominally encode the work register count in [7:0]
-    * and the scoreboard slot count in [15:8], but the encoding is not
-    * reliable across kbase/firmware versions (a Pixel 7 kbase 1.38
-    * reports a work register value that underflows the register file the
-    * CS compiler was designed for).  Mirror the panthor kernel driver
-    * instead, which hardcodes 96 CS registers with 4 unpreserved ones on
-    * all shipping CSF parts, and only trust the scoreboard count when it
-    * is in the plausible [PANVK-supported] 1..16 range. */
+   /* Stream features encode the work register count in [7:0] as WORK_REGS-1
+    * and the scoreboard slot count in [15:8], the same encoding panthor's
+    * kernel driver reads.  Some kernels (a Pixel 7 kbase 1.38) report a stale
+    * value below the register file the CS compiler was sized against, so 96 -
+    * what panthor assumes on every shipping CSF part below 5th-gen - is the
+    * floor.  From 5th-gen the file is 128 and the reported value is what says
+    * so, so take the larger of the two and cap it there.  Only trust the
+    * scoreboard count when it is in the plausible [PANVK-supported] 1..16
+    * range. */
    uint32_t stream_features = streams[0].features;
    uint32_t scoreboard_slot_count = (stream_features >> 8) & 0xff;
 
@@ -516,7 +517,7 @@ kbase_query_csif_info(int fd, struct drm_panthor_csif_info *csif)
    *csif = (struct drm_panthor_csif_info){
       .csg_slot_count = group_num,
       .cs_slot_count = groups[0].stream_num,
-      .cs_reg_count = 96,
+      .cs_reg_count = CLAMP((stream_features & 0xff) + 1, 96, 128),
       .scoreboard_slot_count =
          (scoreboard_slot_count - 1) < 16 ? scoreboard_slot_count : 8,
       /* Number of CS registers the FW may clobber; matches panthor's
diff --git a/src/panfrost/vulkan/csf/panvk_vX_gpu_queue.c b/src/panfrost/vulkan/csf/panvk_vX_gpu_queue.c
index 65cb569..138f468 100644
--- a/src/panfrost/vulkan/csf/panvk_vX_gpu_queue.c
+++ b/src/panfrost/vulkan/csf/panvk_vX_gpu_queue.c
@@ -492,6 +492,7 @@ kbase_subqueue_emit_job(struct panvk_gpu_queue *queue, uint32_t subqueue,
    };
    struct cs_builder_conf conf = {
       .nr_registers = csif_info->cs_reg_count,
+      .nr_kernel_registers = MAX2(csif_info->unpreserved_cs_reg_count, 4),
       .ls_sb_slot = SB_ID(LS),
    };
    struct cs_builder b;
